window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-111"]=[{"id":"K111.01","q":"111:1–5","s":111,"surah":"al-Masad","surah_ar":"المسد","title":"al-Masad · Heading, verses, and destruction","cairo":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","source_page_first":813,"source_page_last":816,"chunk":"passages-111","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Heading, verses, and destruction [IV:813]","html":"<p>Meccan; five verses. [The DKA heading places it after al-Fātiḥa.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>May Abū Lahab’s hands perish—and perish he did! (1) His wealth and what he earned have availed him nothing. (2) He shall burn in a flaming fire, (3) and his wife, the carrier of firewood, (4) with a rope of twisted fibre about her neck. (5)</p><p>Tabāb means destruction. Hence their question, “Is she young, or tābba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تابّة</bdi>)?”—worn out by decrepitude and old age. His hands perish because, according to a report, he picked up a stone to throw at God’s Messenger; “and he perished” means his whole person perished. Or his hands are declared ruined with the destruction of his entire person intended, as in “because of what your two hands sent ahead” [22:10]. “And he perished” then means this occurred and was accomplished, as in:\n“He requited me—may God requite him with the worst requital—\nwith the requital of howling dogs; and He has done so.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K111.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Loss and old age [XVI:622]","html":"<p>Meccan; five verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “tabāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تباب</bdi>) means destruction,” al-Rāghib says: “Tabb and tabāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تباب</bdi>) mean persistence in loss. One says tabban lahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تَبًّا له</bdi>) and tabbun lahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تَبٌّ له</bdi>); tabbabtuhu means I said that to him. Because persistence is included, istatabba li-fulān means something became established for him. ‘Abū Lahab’s hands perished’ means they persisted in loss; ‘they added only destruction to them’ [11:101] means causing loss.” On “becoming aged”: ʿajazat (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَجَزَتْ</bdi>) and ʿajjazat (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَجَّزَتْ</bdi>) describe a woman becoming old, just as tathayyabat means becoming previously married.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Hands, the whole person, and certainty [XVI:623–624]","html":"<p>On “his entire person is meant,” compare:\n“A man whose hands begrudge another man\na benefit from someone else is surely miserly.”\nThat is, he begrudges him. Al-Jawharī: “‘This is what your hands have committed’ means what you committed.”</p><p>On “‘and he perished’: it happened”: according to one explanation tabba is an imprecation in the first interpretation and a statement in the second; tabbat is an imprecation in either. The Imām says the first can mean the destruction of his deeds, the second of himself: a man strives for his own welfare and that of his work, and God declares him deprived of both.</p><p>I say: the composition supports this. What follows explains it: “His wealth and earnings availed him nothing” points to his deeds’ destruction, and “He shall burn in a flaming fire” to his own. The past tense first signals certainty, as God’s announcements of future events do; the future tense second depicts the coming state before the listener’s eyes. Ibn Masʿūd’s “he has indeed perished” supports this, since qad confirms, as in the poet’s “and He has done so.” The poem means: he requited me like howling dogs—“running dogs” is also transmitted—may God give him His worst requital, and He has already done it.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 5 [نزلت بعد الفاتحة] بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nالتباب: الهلاك. ومنه قولهم: أشابة أم تابة، أى: هالكة من الهرم والتعجيز. والمعنى: هلكت يداه، لأنه فيما يروى: أخذ حجرا ليرمى به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وتب وهلك كله. أو جعلت يداه هالكتين. والمراد: هلاك جملته، كقوله تعالى بما قدمت يداك ومعنى وتب: وكان ذلك وحصل، كقوله: جزانى جزاه الله شر جزائه... جزاء الكلاب العاويات وقد فعل\n\nمكية، وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (التباب: الهلاك)، الراغب: «التَّبُّ والتَّباب: الاستمرار في الخسران، يقال: تبًّا له وتبٌّ له وتَببْتُه: إذا قلتُ له ذلك، ولتضمّن ِالاستمرار قيل: استتبَّ لفلانٍ كذا، أي: استمرَّ. و \" تَبّتْ يدا أبي لهب\"، أي: استمرت في الخُسران، قال الله تعالى: ﴿وَمَا زَادُوهُمْ غَيْرَ تَتْبِيبٍ﴾ [هود: 101]، أي: تخسير».\n\nقولهُ: (والتَّعْجيز)، عن بعضهم: عَجَزَتِ المرأةُ وعَجّزتْ: إذا صارت عجوزًا، كما تقول: تَثيّبتِ المرأة، إذا صارت ثَيِّبة.\n\nقولُه: (والمراد: هلاك جُملته)، ونحوه قول الشاعر:\n\nوإنّ امرءًا ضَنَّتْ يَداهُ على امرئٍ بِنَيْلِ يدٍ من غيرِه لبخيلُ\n\nأي: ضنّ على امرئٍ. الجوهري: «يُقال: هذا ما جَنَتْ يداك، أي: جَنَيت».\n\nقولُه: (ومعنى ﴿وَتَبَّ﴾: وكان ذلك وحَصَل)، عن بعضهم: فتَبَّ على الأولِ: دعاءٌ، وعلى الثاني: خبر. و «تَبَّتْ» دعاءٌ على كلِّ حال. قال الإمام: «يجوزُ أن يراد بالأولِ هلاكُ عملِه، وبالثاني هلاكُ نفسِه، ووجهُهُ أن المرءَ إنما يسعى لمصلحةِ نفسِه وعملِه، فأخبرَ اللهُ تعالى أنه محرومٌ من الأمرين».\n\nوقلتُ: النظمُ يساعدُ قولَ الإمام، لأن ما بعدَه بيانٌ وتفسير؛ فإنّ قولَه: ﴿مَا أَغْنَى عَنْهُ مَالُهُ وَمَا كَسَبَ﴾، إشارةٌ إلى هلاكِ عملِه، وقولَه: ﴿سَيَصْلَى نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ﴾، إشارةٌ إلى هلاكِ نفسِه. وقال «تَبَّ» أولًا على الماضي، ليؤذنَ بالقطعِ على سننِ إخبارِ الله عن المستقبل، و ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾ ثانيًا على الاستقبال، حكايةً للحالِ الآتية، تصويرًا لها في مشاهدةِ السامع.\n\nيؤيدُه أيضًا قراءةُ ابنِ مسعود رضي الله عنه: «وقد تَبَّ»، لأن «قد» للتحقيق كما في قول الشاعر.\n\nوقد فَعَلْ.\n\nتقديره: جَزاني جزاء الكلابِ العاويات، ويروى: العاديات، جزاهُ اللهُ شَرَّ جزائِه وقد فعلَ ذلك، أي: كانَ ذلك وقد حَصَل.","layers":[{"id":"K111.01","heading":"K111.01 — Heading, verses, and destruction [IV:813]","text":"Meccan; five verses. [The DKA heading places it after al-Fātiḥa.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nMay Abū Lahab’s hands perish—and perish he did! (1) His wealth and what he earned have availed him nothing. (2) He shall burn in a flaming fire, (3) and his wife, the carrier of firewood, (4) with a rope of twisted fibre about her neck. (5)\n\nTabāb means destruction. Hence their question, “Is she young, or tābba (تابّة)?”—worn out by decrepitude and old age. His hands perish because, according to a report, he picked up a stone to throw at God’s Messenger; “and he perished” means his whole person perished. Or his hands are declared ruined with the destruction of his entire person intended, as in “because of what your two hands sent ahead” [22:10]. “And he perished” then means this occurred and was accomplished, as in:\n“He requited me—may God requite him with the worst requital—\nwith the requital of howling dogs; and He has done so.”","title":"Heading, verses, and destruction [IV:813]","author":"kashshaf","surah":111,"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 5 [نزلت بعد الفاتحة] بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nالتباب: الهلاك. ومنه قولهم: أشابة أم تابة، أى: هالكة من الهرم والتعجيز. والمعنى: هلكت يداه، لأنه فيما يروى: أخذ حجرا ليرمى به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وتب وهلك كله. أو جعلت يداه هالكتين. والمراد: هلاك جملته، كقوله تعالى بما قدمت يداك ومعنى وتب: وكان ذلك وحصل، كقوله: جزانى جزاه الله شر جزائه... جزاء الكلاب العاويات وقد فعل","html":"<p>Meccan; five verses. [The DKA heading places it after al-Fātiḥa.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>May Abū Lahab’s hands perish—and perish he did! (1) His wealth and what he earned have availed him nothing. (2) He shall burn in a flaming fire, (3) and his wife, the carrier of firewood, (4) with a rope of twisted fibre about her neck. (5)</p><p>Tabāb means destruction. Hence their question, “Is she young, or tābba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تابّة</bdi>)?”—worn out by decrepitude and old age. His hands perish because, according to a report, he picked up a stone to throw at God’s Messenger; “and he perished” means his whole person perished. Or his hands are declared ruined with the destruction of his entire person intended, as in “because of what your two hands sent ahead” [22:10]. “And he perished” then means this occurred and was accomplished, as in:\n“He requited me—may God requite him with the worst requital—\nwith the requital of howling dogs; and He has done so.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.01","heading":"T111.01 — Loss and old age [XVI:622]","text":"Meccan; five verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nOn “tabāb (تباب) means destruction,” al-Rāghib says: “Tabb and tabāb (تباب) mean persistence in loss. One says tabban lahu (تَبًّا له) and tabbun lahu (تَبٌّ له); tabbabtuhu means I said that to him. Because persistence is included, istatabba li-fulān means something became established for him. ‘Abū Lahab’s hands perished’ means they persisted in loss; ‘they added only destruction to them’ [11:101] means causing loss.” On “becoming aged”: ʿajazat (عَجَزَتْ) and ʿajjazat (عَجَّزَتْ) describe a woman becoming old, just as tathayyabat means becoming previously married.","title":"Loss and old age [XVI:622]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.01"],"anchor":"K111.01","surah":111,"ar":"مكية، وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (التباب: الهلاك)، الراغب: «التَّبُّ والتَّباب: الاستمرار في الخسران، يقال: تبًّا له وتبٌّ له وتَببْتُه: إذا قلتُ له ذلك، ولتضمّن ِالاستمرار قيل: استتبَّ لفلانٍ كذا، أي: استمرَّ. و \" تَبّتْ يدا أبي لهب\"، أي: استمرت في الخُسران، قال الله تعالى: ﴿وَمَا زَادُوهُمْ غَيْرَ تَتْبِيبٍ﴾ [هود: 101]، أي: تخسير».\n\nقولهُ: (والتَّعْجيز)، عن بعضهم: عَجَزَتِ المرأةُ وعَجّزتْ: إذا صارت عجوزًا، كما تقول: تَثيّبتِ المرأة، إذا صارت ثَيِّبة.","html":"<p>Meccan; five verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “tabāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تباب</bdi>) means destruction,” al-Rāghib says: “Tabb and tabāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تباب</bdi>) mean persistence in loss. One says tabban lahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تَبًّا له</bdi>) and tabbun lahu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تَبٌّ له</bdi>); tabbabtuhu means I said that to him. Because persistence is included, istatabba li-fulān means something became established for him. ‘Abū Lahab’s hands perished’ means they persisted in loss; ‘they added only destruction to them’ [11:101] means causing loss.” On “becoming aged”: ʿajazat (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَجَزَتْ</bdi>) and ʿajjazat (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عَجَّزَتْ</bdi>) describe a woman becoming old, just as tathayyabat means becoming previously married.</p>","lemma":"التباب: الهلاك","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.02","heading":"T111.02 — Hands, the whole person, and certainty [XVI:623–624]","text":"On “his entire person is meant,” compare:\n“A man whose hands begrudge another man\na benefit from someone else is surely miserly.”\nThat is, he begrudges him. Al-Jawharī: “‘This is what your hands have committed’ means what you committed.”\n\nOn “‘and he perished’: it happened”: according to one explanation tabba is an imprecation in the first interpretation and a statement in the second; tabbat is an imprecation in either. The Imām says the first can mean the destruction of his deeds, the second of himself: a man strives for his own welfare and that of his work, and God declares him deprived of both.\n\nI say: the composition supports this. What follows explains it: “His wealth and earnings availed him nothing” points to his deeds’ destruction, and “He shall burn in a flaming fire” to his own. The past tense first signals certainty, as God’s announcements of future events do; the future tense second depicts the coming state before the listener’s eyes. Ibn Masʿūd’s “he has indeed perished” supports this, since qad confirms, as in the poet’s “and He has done so.” The poem means: he requited me like howling dogs—“running dogs” is also transmitted—may God give him His worst requital, and He has already done it.","title":"Hands, the whole person, and certainty [XVI:623–624]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.01"],"anchor":"K111.01","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (والمراد: هلاك جُملته)، ونحوه قول الشاعر:\n\nوإنّ امرءًا ضَنَّتْ يَداهُ على امرئٍ بِنَيْلِ يدٍ من غيرِه لبخيلُ\n\nأي: ضنّ على امرئٍ. الجوهري: «يُقال: هذا ما جَنَتْ يداك، أي: جَنَيت».\n\nقولُه: (ومعنى ﴿وَتَبَّ﴾: وكان ذلك وحَصَل)، عن بعضهم: فتَبَّ على الأولِ: دعاءٌ، وعلى الثاني: خبر. و «تَبَّتْ» دعاءٌ على كلِّ حال. قال الإمام: «يجوزُ أن يراد بالأولِ هلاكُ عملِه، وبالثاني هلاكُ نفسِه، ووجهُهُ أن المرءَ إنما يسعى لمصلحةِ نفسِه وعملِه، فأخبرَ اللهُ تعالى أنه محرومٌ من الأمرين».\n\nوقلتُ: النظمُ يساعدُ قولَ الإمام، لأن ما بعدَه بيانٌ وتفسير؛ فإنّ قولَه: ﴿مَا أَغْنَى عَنْهُ مَالُهُ وَمَا كَسَبَ﴾، إشارةٌ إلى هلاكِ عملِه، وقولَه: ﴿سَيَصْلَى نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ﴾، إشارةٌ إلى هلاكِ نفسِه. وقال «تَبَّ» أولًا على الماضي، ليؤذنَ بالقطعِ على سننِ إخبارِ الله عن المستقبل، و ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾ ثانيًا على الاستقبال، حكايةً للحالِ الآتية، تصويرًا لها في مشاهدةِ السامع.\n\nيؤيدُه أيضًا قراءةُ ابنِ مسعود رضي الله عنه: «وقد تَبَّ»، لأن «قد» للتحقيق كما في قول الشاعر.\n\nوقد فَعَلْ.\n\nتقديره: جَزاني جزاء الكلابِ العاويات، ويروى: العاديات، جزاهُ اللهُ شَرَّ جزائِه وقد فعلَ ذلك، أي: كانَ ذلك وقد حَصَل.","html":"<p>On “his entire person is meant,” compare:\n“A man whose hands begrudge another man\na benefit from someone else is surely miserly.”\nThat is, he begrudges him. Al-Jawharī: “‘This is what your hands have committed’ means what you committed.”</p><p>On “‘and he perished’: it happened”: according to one explanation tabba is an imprecation in the first interpretation and a statement in the second; tabbat is an imprecation in either. The Imām says the first can mean the destruction of his deeds, the second of himself: a man strives for his own welfare and that of his work, and God declares him deprived of both.</p><p>I say: the composition supports this. What follows explains it: “His wealth and earnings availed him nothing” points to his deeds’ destruction, and “He shall burn in a flaming fire” to his own. The past tense first signals certainty, as God’s announcements of future events do; the future tense second depicts the coming state before the listener’s eyes. Ibn Masʿūd’s “he has indeed perished” supports this, since qad confirms, as in the poet’s “and He has done so.” The poem means: he requited me like howling dogs—“running dogs” is also transmitted—may God give him His worst requital, and He has already done it.</p>","lemma":"والمراد: هلاك جملته","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"111:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ تَبَّتْ يَدَآ أَبِى لَهَبٍ وَتَبَّ","en":"The power of Abu Lahab will perish, and he will perish"},{"key":"111:2","number":2,"ar":"مَآ أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥ وَمَا كَسَبَ","en":"His wealth and gains will not exempt him"},{"key":"111:3","number":3,"ar":"سَيَصْلَىٰ نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ","en":"He will be plunged in flaming Fire"},{"key":"111:4","number":4,"ar":"وَٱمْرَأَتُهُۥ حَمَّالَةَ ٱلْحَطَبِ","en":"And his wife, the wood-carrier"},{"key":"111:5","number":5,"ar":"فِى جِيدِهَا حَبْلٌ مِّن مَّسَدٍۭ","en":"Will have upon her neck a halter of palm-fibre"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ تَبَّتْ يَدَآ أَبِى لَهَبٍ وَتَبَّ ﴿1﴾ مَآ أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥ وَمَا كَسَبَ ﴿2﴾ سَيَصْلَىٰ نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ ﴿3﴾ وَٱمْرَأَتُهُۥ حَمَّالَةَ ٱلْحَطَبِ ﴿4﴾ فِى جِيدِهَا حَبْلٌ مِّن مَّسَدٍۭ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"1. The power of Abu Lahab will perish, and he will perish 2. His wealth and gains will not exempt him 3. He will be plunged in flaming Fire 4. And his wife, the wood-carrier 5. Will have upon her neck a halter of palm-fibre"},{"id":"K111.02","q":"111:1","s":111,"surah":"al-Masad","surah_ar":"المسد","title":"al-Masad · The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname","cairo":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","source_page_first":813,"source_page_last":816,"chunk":"passages-111","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname [IV:814]","html":"<p>Ibn Masʿūd’s reading “and he has indeed perished” supports this. It is related that when “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] was revealed, he climbed al-Ṣafā and cried, “Alarm!” People gathered from every direction. He said, “Sons of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, sons of Fihr: if I told you there was cavalry at this mountain’s foot, would you believe me?” “Yes.” “Then I warn you before the Hour.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you! Is this why you summoned us?” The sūrah was revealed.</p><p>If you ask why he is given his kunya, when this is an honour, I reply: there are three explanations. First, he may have been known by his kunya rather than his name. A man may be known by one rather than the other; hence either may follow the other as explanatory apposition. Since the intention was to make him notorious by a curse that would remain his mark, the better-known designation was used. This is supported by the reading yadā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يدا</bdi>) Abū Lahab, like “ʿAlī ibn Abū Ṭālib” and “Muʿāwiya ibn Abū Sufyān”: nothing is altered in the familiar name, lest the listener become confused. Falīta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة</bdi>) ibn Qāsim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة بن قاسم</bdi>), Mecca’s ruler, had two sons, one called ʿAbdi-llāh with the genitive, the other ʿAbda-llāh with the accusative. There was a man in Mecca known only as ʿAbdi-llāh, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the dāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">د</bdi>).</p><p>Second, his name was ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, so the kunya was used instead. Third, he belonged to the people of the Fire and would end in a flaming fire, so his condition suited his kunya, “Father of Flame,” making it fitting. One calls someone Abū Lahab as one calls a wicked person “Father of Evil” and a good person “Father of Good,” or as the Messenger called al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra because of yellow colouring in his face. It is also said that his cheeks glowed and shone, which gave him the name; using it may therefore mock both him and his pride in it.</p><p>Abī Lahb is also read with quiescent hāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ه</bdi>): an alteration peculiar to proper names, like Shums ibn Mālik with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>).</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K111.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The cry of warning [XVI:624]","html":"<p>The report upon “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] is transmitted by al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Imām Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Prophet climbed al-Ṣafā, calling the branches of Quraysh, “Sons of Fihr! Sons of ʿAdī!” They gathered; anyone unable to come sent someone to discover the matter. Abū Lahab and Quraysh came. He said, “If I told you cavalry in the valley intended to raid you, would you believe me?” “Yes: we have experienced only truthfulness from you.” “Then I warn you before a severe punishment.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you all day! Is this why you gathered us?” The sūrah descended.</p><p>Al-Nihāya explains yā ṣabāḥāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صباحاه</bdi>): “A cry for aid, originally the alarm for a raid, since raids commonly occurred in the morning: morning has come, so prepare.” Safḥ is the mountain’s lower slope, where water pours down.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Names, readings, and al-Muhallab [XVI:625–626]","html":"<p>On preserving the name unchanged, al-Intiṣāf says: “This indicates that the nominative precedes the other case forms. They preserve its familiar shape; the name became known thus. His name ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā was avoided in favour of his kunya because it was disliked.”</p><p>Falīta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة</bdi>) has fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>); Fukayta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُكَيْتة</bdi>) with kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) and the diminutive is also transmitted. The report that the Messenger named al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra is not mentioned in Jāmiʿ al-uṣūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأصول</bdi>). Al-Muhallab is Abū Saʿīd al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufra; his father’s name was Ẓālim ibn Surrāq ibn Ṣubayḥ al-Azdī. Al-Muhallab fought the celebrated wars against the Khārijites and died in 83 at Marw al-Rūdh under ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān. He belongs to the first generation of Basran Successors and saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.</p><p>“It is also said he was so named” subdivides the third explanation; it is not a fourth. The kunya is preferred either because it uniquely identifies him and his name would confuse; or both identify him equally and the kunya was chosen though the name was possible; or for a subtle point: to signify that he belongs to Hell, either simply or with mockery. The author of al-Miftāḥ indicates the first explanation and the first version of the third.</p><p>Ibn Kathīr reads Lahb with quiescent hāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ه</bdi>); the others with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). Abū l-Baqāʾ calls them two dialectal forms. Mā’s accusative position is as an absolute object: “What benefit?” Abū l-Baqāʾ gives the two interpretations and says it cannot mean “that which.” The author is reported to have said: māl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مال</bdi>) is general; Bedouins use it of camels, while their landed notables use it of an estate.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.04"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ويدل عليه قراءة ابن مسعود: وقد تب، وروى أنه لما نزل وأنذر عشيرتك الأقربين رقى الصفا وقال. يا صباحاه، فاستجمع إليه الناس من كل أوب. فقال: يا بنى عبد المطلب، يا بنى فهر، إن أخبرتكم أن بسفح هذا الجبل خيلا أكنتم مصدقى؟ قالوا: نعم، قال: فإنى نذير لكم بين يدي الساعة، فقال أبو لهب: تبا لك، ألهذا دعوتنا ؟ فنزلت. فإن قلت: لم كناه، والتكنية تكرمة؟ قلت: فيه ثلاثة أوجه، أحدها: أن يكون مشتهرا بالكنية دون الاسم، فقد يكون الرجل معروفا بأحدهما، ولذلك تجرى الكنية على الاسم، أو الاسم على الكنية عطف بيان، فلما أريد تشهيره بدعوة السوء وأن تبقى سمة له، ذكر الأشهر من علميه ويؤيد ذلك قراءة من قرأ، يدا أبو لهب، كما قيل، على بن أبو طالب. ومعاوية بن أبو سفيان، لئلا يغير منه شيء فيشكل على السامع، ولفليتة بن قاسم أمير مكة ابنان، أحدهما: عبد الله- بالجر، والآخر عبد الله- بالنصب. كان بمكة رجل يقال له: عبد الله- بجرة الدال، لا يعرف إلا هكذا. والثاني: أنه كان اسمه عبد العزى، فعدل عنه إلى كنيته. والثالث: أنه لما كان من أهل النار ومآله إلى نار ذات لهب، وافقت حاله كنيته، فكان جديرا بأن يذكر بها. ويقال: أبو لهب، كما يقال: أبو الشر للشرير. وأبو الخير للخير، وكما كنى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أبا المهلب: أبا صفرة، بصفرة في وجهه. وقيل كنى بذلك لتلهب وجنتيه وإشراقهما، فيجوز أن يذكر بذلك تهكما به، وبافتخاره بذلك. وقرئ أبى لهب، بالسكون. وهو من تغيير الأعلام، كقولهم: شمس بن مالك بالضم\n\nقولُه: (وروي أنه لمّا نزل ﴿وَأَنْذِرْ عَشِيرَتَكَ الْأَقْرَبِينَ﴾ [الشعراء: 214])، الحديث [من] رواية البخاريُّ ومسلمٍ والإمام أحمد َ والترمذيِّ، عن ابنِ عباس، قال: \" لمّا نزلتْ: ﴿وَأَنْذِرْ عَشِيرَتَكَ الْأَقْرَبِينَ﴾، صعِدَ النبيُّ صلى الله عليه وسلم على الصَّفا، فجعلَ ينادي: يا بني فِهْر، يا بني عديّ، لبطونِ قريش، حتى اجتمعوا، فجعلَ الرجلُ إذا لم يستطعْ أن يخرجَ، أرسلَ رسولًا لينظرَ ما هو، فجاءَ أبو لهبِ وقريش.\n\nفقال: أرأيتكم لو أخبرتكم أن خيلًا بالوادي تريدُ أن تغيرَ عليكم، كنتم مصدِّقيّ؟ قالوا: نعم، ما جَربّنا عليك إلا صدقًا. قال: فإني نذيرٌ لكم بين يَدَيْ عذابٍ شديد. فقالَ أبو لهب: تَبًّا لك سائرَ اليوم، ألهذا جمعتنا؟ فنزلتْ.\n\nقولُه: (يا صَباحاه)، النهاية «هذه كلمةٌ [يقولُها] المستغيث، وأصلها: إذا صاحوا للغارة؛ لأنهم أكثرُ ما كانوا يُغيرونَ عند الصباح، فكأنه يريد: قد جاءَ الصباحُ فتأهبوا».\n\nقولُه: (بسَفْحِ هذا الجبل)، سَفْحُ الجبلِ: أسفلُه، حيثُ يُسفحُ فيه الماء.\n\nقولُه: (لئلا يُغيّرَ منه شئٌ فيشكلُ على السامع)، \"الانتصاف\": «وفيهِ دليلٌ على أن الرّفعَ أسبقُ [وجوهِ] الإعراب، ألا تراهم حافظوا على صورتِه وصيغته، فاشتُهرَ الاسمُ بهذا، وعُدلَ عن اسمِه عبد العُزّى إلى كُنْيتِه لكراهيتِه».\n\nقولُه: (ولِفلِيتةَ)، فَلِيتة: بالفاء المفتوحةِ واللام المكسورة، ويُروى: (ولفُكَيْتة) بالكافِ والتصغير.\n\nقولُه: (وكما كنّي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أبا المهلَّب: أبا صُفرة)، وليسَ في \"جامعِ الأصولِ\" له ذِكْر. وأما المهلَّبُ، فهو أبو سعيدٍ، المهلّبُ بنُ أبي صُفرة. وأبو صُفرة اسمُه ظالمُ بن سَرَّاق بنِ صبيح الأزدي. ومهلَّبُ صاحبُ الحروبِ المشهورة مع الخوارجِ، مات سنةَ ثلاثٍ وثمانين بمّرْو الُّروذ، في أيام [عبد] الملك بنِ مروان، وهو من الطبقةِ الأولى من تابعي البصرة، رأى عمرَ بن الخطاب رضي الله عنه.\n\nقولُه: (وقيل: كُنّي بذلك)، هذا قسيمٌ للوجه الثالث وليسَ بوجهٍ رابع، يعني: أوثرتِ الكنيةُ إما لاشتهارِه بها واختصاصِها به، حتى إنه لو سُمّي لالتبس، أو إنهما سِيِّان، فَعُدلَ إلى الكنيةِ ولو سُمّي لجاز، أو عُدل إليها رعايةُ لنكتة، وهي إما لأنه يكنى بها، أنه جَهَنّميّ، كنايةَ مُجّردة أو مع التهكّم. وقد أشارَ صاحبُ \"المفتاح\" إلى الوجه الأول، والأولِ من الثالث.\n\nقولُه: (وقرئ: \"أبي لَهْبٍ\" بالسكون)، ابنُ كثير، والباقونَ: بفتحِ الهاء. قالَ أبو البقاء: \" ﴿لَهَبٍ﴾ [بالفتحِ] والإسكانِ لغتان\".\n\nقولُه: (ومَحَلُّه النصب)، أي على أنه مفعولٌ مطلق، أَي: أيَّ غناء. ذكرَ أبو البقاءِ الوجهين، وقال: \"ما\" لا يكونُ بمعنى \"الذي\". رُوي عن المصنف: المالُ اسمٌ عام؛ فعندَ أهلِ البدوِ استعملَ في الإبل، وعند دَهاقتِهم في الضّيعة.","layers":[{"id":"K111.02","heading":"K111.02 — The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname [IV:814]","text":"Ibn Masʿūd’s reading “and he has indeed perished” supports this. It is related that when “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] was revealed, he climbed al-Ṣafā and cried, “Alarm!” People gathered from every direction. He said, “Sons of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, sons of Fihr: if I told you there was cavalry at this mountain’s foot, would you believe me?” “Yes.” “Then I warn you before the Hour.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you! Is this why you summoned us?” The sūrah was revealed.\n\nIf you ask why he is given his kunya, when this is an honour, I reply: there are three explanations. First, he may have been known by his kunya rather than his name. A man may be known by one rather than the other; hence either may follow the other as explanatory apposition. Since the intention was to make him notorious by a curse that would remain his mark, the better-known designation was used. This is supported by the reading yadā (يدا) Abū Lahab, like “ʿAlī ibn Abū Ṭālib” and “Muʿāwiya ibn Abū Sufyān”: nothing is altered in the familiar name, lest the listener become confused. Falīta (فَلِيتة) ibn Qāsim (فَلِيتة بن قاسم), Mecca’s ruler, had two sons, one called ʿAbdi-llāh with the genitive, the other ʿAbda-llāh with the accusative. There was a man in Mecca known only as ʿAbdi-llāh, with kasra (كَسْرة) on the dāl (د).\n\nSecond, his name was ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, so the kunya was used instead. Third, he belonged to the people of the Fire and would end in a flaming fire, so his condition suited his kunya, “Father of Flame,” making it fitting. One calls someone Abū Lahab as one calls a wicked person “Father of Evil” and a good person “Father of Good,” or as the Messenger called al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra because of yellow colouring in his face. It is also said that his cheeks glowed and shone, which gave him the name; using it may therefore mock both him and his pride in it.\n\nAbī Lahb is also read with quiescent hāʾ (ه): an alteration peculiar to proper names, like Shums ibn Mālik with ḍamma (ضَمّة).","title":"The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname [IV:814]","author":"kashshaf","surah":111,"ar":"ويدل عليه قراءة ابن مسعود: وقد تب، وروى أنه لما نزل وأنذر عشيرتك الأقربين رقى الصفا وقال. يا صباحاه، فاستجمع إليه الناس من كل أوب. فقال: يا بنى عبد المطلب، يا بنى فهر، إن أخبرتكم أن بسفح هذا الجبل خيلا أكنتم مصدقى؟ قالوا: نعم، قال: فإنى نذير لكم بين يدي الساعة، فقال أبو لهب: تبا لك، ألهذا دعوتنا ؟ فنزلت. فإن قلت: لم كناه، والتكنية تكرمة؟ قلت: فيه ثلاثة أوجه، أحدها: أن يكون مشتهرا بالكنية دون الاسم، فقد يكون الرجل معروفا بأحدهما، ولذلك تجرى الكنية على الاسم، أو الاسم على الكنية عطف بيان، فلما أريد تشهيره بدعوة السوء وأن تبقى سمة له، ذكر الأشهر من علميه ويؤيد ذلك قراءة من قرأ، يدا أبو لهب، كما قيل، على بن أبو طالب. ومعاوية بن أبو سفيان، لئلا يغير منه شيء فيشكل على السامع، ولفليتة بن قاسم أمير مكة ابنان، أحدهما: عبد الله- بالجر، والآخر عبد الله- بالنصب. كان بمكة رجل يقال له: عبد الله- بجرة الدال، لا يعرف إلا هكذا. والثاني: أنه كان اسمه عبد العزى، فعدل عنه إلى كنيته. والثالث: أنه لما كان من أهل النار ومآله إلى نار ذات لهب، وافقت حاله كنيته، فكان جديرا بأن يذكر بها. ويقال: أبو لهب، كما يقال: أبو الشر للشرير. وأبو الخير للخير، وكما كنى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أبا المهلب: أبا صفرة، بصفرة في وجهه. وقيل كنى بذلك لتلهب وجنتيه وإشراقهما، فيجوز أن يذكر بذلك تهكما به، وبافتخاره بذلك. وقرئ أبى لهب، بالسكون. وهو من تغيير الأعلام، كقولهم: شمس بن مالك بالضم","html":"<p>Ibn Masʿūd’s reading “and he has indeed perished” supports this. It is related that when “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] was revealed, he climbed al-Ṣafā and cried, “Alarm!” People gathered from every direction. He said, “Sons of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, sons of Fihr: if I told you there was cavalry at this mountain’s foot, would you believe me?” “Yes.” “Then I warn you before the Hour.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you! Is this why you summoned us?” The sūrah was revealed.</p><p>If you ask why he is given his kunya, when this is an honour, I reply: there are three explanations. First, he may have been known by his kunya rather than his name. A man may be known by one rather than the other; hence either may follow the other as explanatory apposition. Since the intention was to make him notorious by a curse that would remain his mark, the better-known designation was used. This is supported by the reading yadā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يدا</bdi>) Abū Lahab, like “ʿAlī ibn Abū Ṭālib” and “Muʿāwiya ibn Abū Sufyān”: nothing is altered in the familiar name, lest the listener become confused. Falīta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة</bdi>) ibn Qāsim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة بن قاسم</bdi>), Mecca’s ruler, had two sons, one called ʿAbdi-llāh with the genitive, the other ʿAbda-llāh with the accusative. There was a man in Mecca known only as ʿAbdi-llāh, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the dāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">د</bdi>).</p><p>Second, his name was ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, so the kunya was used instead. Third, he belonged to the people of the Fire and would end in a flaming fire, so his condition suited his kunya, “Father of Flame,” making it fitting. One calls someone Abū Lahab as one calls a wicked person “Father of Evil” and a good person “Father of Good,” or as the Messenger called al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra because of yellow colouring in his face. It is also said that his cheeks glowed and shone, which gave him the name; using it may therefore mock both him and his pride in it.</p><p>Abī Lahb is also read with quiescent hāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ه</bdi>): an alteration peculiar to proper names, like Shums ibn Mālik with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>).</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.03","heading":"T111.03 — The cry of warning [XVI:624]","text":"The report upon “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] is transmitted by al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Imām Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Prophet climbed al-Ṣafā, calling the branches of Quraysh, “Sons of Fihr! Sons of ʿAdī!” They gathered; anyone unable to come sent someone to discover the matter. Abū Lahab and Quraysh came. He said, “If I told you cavalry in the valley intended to raid you, would you believe me?” “Yes: we have experienced only truthfulness from you.” “Then I warn you before a severe punishment.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you all day! Is this why you gathered us?” The sūrah descended.\n\nAl-Nihāya explains yā ṣabāḥāh (صباحاه): “A cry for aid, originally the alarm for a raid, since raids commonly occurred in the morning: morning has come, so prepare.” Safḥ is the mountain’s lower slope, where water pours down.","title":"The cry of warning [XVI:624]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.02"],"anchor":"K111.02","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (وروي أنه لمّا نزل ﴿وَأَنْذِرْ عَشِيرَتَكَ الْأَقْرَبِينَ﴾ [الشعراء: 214])، الحديث [من] رواية البخاريُّ ومسلمٍ والإمام أحمد َ والترمذيِّ، عن ابنِ عباس، قال: \" لمّا نزلتْ: ﴿وَأَنْذِرْ عَشِيرَتَكَ الْأَقْرَبِينَ﴾، صعِدَ النبيُّ صلى الله عليه وسلم على الصَّفا، فجعلَ ينادي: يا بني فِهْر، يا بني عديّ، لبطونِ قريش، حتى اجتمعوا، فجعلَ الرجلُ إذا لم يستطعْ أن يخرجَ، أرسلَ رسولًا لينظرَ ما هو، فجاءَ أبو لهبِ وقريش.\n\nفقال: أرأيتكم لو أخبرتكم أن خيلًا بالوادي تريدُ أن تغيرَ عليكم، كنتم مصدِّقيّ؟ قالوا: نعم، ما جَربّنا عليك إلا صدقًا. قال: فإني نذيرٌ لكم بين يَدَيْ عذابٍ شديد. فقالَ أبو لهب: تَبًّا لك سائرَ اليوم، ألهذا جمعتنا؟ فنزلتْ.\n\nقولُه: (يا صَباحاه)، النهاية «هذه كلمةٌ [يقولُها] المستغيث، وأصلها: إذا صاحوا للغارة؛ لأنهم أكثرُ ما كانوا يُغيرونَ عند الصباح، فكأنه يريد: قد جاءَ الصباحُ فتأهبوا».\n\nقولُه: (بسَفْحِ هذا الجبل)، سَفْحُ الجبلِ: أسفلُه، حيثُ يُسفحُ فيه الماء.","html":"<p>The report upon “Warn your nearest kinsfolk” [26:214] is transmitted by al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Imām Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Prophet climbed al-Ṣafā, calling the branches of Quraysh, “Sons of Fihr! Sons of ʿAdī!” They gathered; anyone unable to come sent someone to discover the matter. Abū Lahab and Quraysh came. He said, “If I told you cavalry in the valley intended to raid you, would you believe me?” “Yes: we have experienced only truthfulness from you.” “Then I warn you before a severe punishment.” Abū Lahab said, “Ruin to you all day! Is this why you gathered us?” The sūrah descended.</p><p>Al-Nihāya explains yā ṣabāḥāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صباحاه</bdi>): “A cry for aid, originally the alarm for a raid, since raids commonly occurred in the morning: morning has come, so prepare.” Safḥ is the mountain’s lower slope, where water pours down.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.04","heading":"T111.04 — Names, readings, and al-Muhallab [XVI:625–626]","text":"On preserving the name unchanged, al-Intiṣāf says: “This indicates that the nominative precedes the other case forms. They preserve its familiar shape; the name became known thus. His name ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā was avoided in favour of his kunya because it was disliked.”\n\nFalīta (فَلِيتة) has fatḥa (فَتْحة) on fāʾ (ف) and kasra (كَسْرة) on lām (ل); Fukayta (فُكَيْتة) with kāf (ك) and the diminutive is also transmitted. The report that the Messenger named al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra is not mentioned in Jāmiʿ al-uṣūl (الأصول). Al-Muhallab is Abū Saʿīd al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufra; his father’s name was Ẓālim ibn Surrāq ibn Ṣubayḥ al-Azdī. Al-Muhallab fought the celebrated wars against the Khārijites and died in 83 at Marw al-Rūdh under ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān. He belongs to the first generation of Basran Successors and saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.\n\n“It is also said he was so named” subdivides the third explanation; it is not a fourth. The kunya is preferred either because it uniquely identifies him and his name would confuse; or both identify him equally and the kunya was chosen though the name was possible; or for a subtle point: to signify that he belongs to Hell, either simply or with mockery. The author of al-Miftāḥ indicates the first explanation and the first version of the third.\n\nIbn Kathīr reads Lahb with quiescent hāʾ (ه); the others with fatḥa (فَتْحة). Abū l-Baqāʾ calls them two dialectal forms. Mā’s accusative position is as an absolute object: “What benefit?” Abū l-Baqāʾ gives the two interpretations and says it cannot mean “that which.” The author is reported to have said: māl (مال) is general; Bedouins use it of camels, while their landed notables use it of an estate.","title":"Names, readings, and al-Muhallab [XVI:625–626]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.02"],"anchor":"K111.02","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (لئلا يُغيّرَ منه شئٌ فيشكلُ على السامع)، \"الانتصاف\": «وفيهِ دليلٌ على أن الرّفعَ أسبقُ [وجوهِ] الإعراب، ألا تراهم حافظوا على صورتِه وصيغته، فاشتُهرَ الاسمُ بهذا، وعُدلَ عن اسمِه عبد العُزّى إلى كُنْيتِه لكراهيتِه».\n\nقولُه: (ولِفلِيتةَ)، فَلِيتة: بالفاء المفتوحةِ واللام المكسورة، ويُروى: (ولفُكَيْتة) بالكافِ والتصغير.\n\nقولُه: (وكما كنّي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أبا المهلَّب: أبا صُفرة)، وليسَ في \"جامعِ الأصولِ\" له ذِكْر. وأما المهلَّبُ، فهو أبو سعيدٍ، المهلّبُ بنُ أبي صُفرة. وأبو صُفرة اسمُه ظالمُ بن سَرَّاق بنِ صبيح الأزدي. ومهلَّبُ صاحبُ الحروبِ المشهورة مع الخوارجِ، مات سنةَ ثلاثٍ وثمانين بمّرْو الُّروذ، في أيام [عبد] الملك بنِ مروان، وهو من الطبقةِ الأولى من تابعي البصرة، رأى عمرَ بن الخطاب رضي الله عنه.\n\nقولُه: (وقيل: كُنّي بذلك)، هذا قسيمٌ للوجه الثالث وليسَ بوجهٍ رابع، يعني: أوثرتِ الكنيةُ إما لاشتهارِه بها واختصاصِها به، حتى إنه لو سُمّي لالتبس، أو إنهما سِيِّان، فَعُدلَ إلى الكنيةِ ولو سُمّي لجاز، أو عُدل إليها رعايةُ لنكتة، وهي إما لأنه يكنى بها، أنه جَهَنّميّ، كنايةَ مُجّردة أو مع التهكّم. وقد أشارَ صاحبُ \"المفتاح\" إلى الوجه الأول، والأولِ من الثالث.\n\nقولُه: (وقرئ: \"أبي لَهْبٍ\" بالسكون)، ابنُ كثير، والباقونَ: بفتحِ الهاء. قالَ أبو البقاء: \" ﴿لَهَبٍ﴾ [بالفتحِ] والإسكانِ لغتان\".\n\nقولُه: (ومَحَلُّه النصب)، أي على أنه مفعولٌ مطلق، أَي: أيَّ غناء. ذكرَ أبو البقاءِ الوجهين، وقال: \"ما\" لا يكونُ بمعنى \"الذي\". رُوي عن المصنف: المالُ اسمٌ عام؛ فعندَ أهلِ البدوِ استعملَ في الإبل، وعند دَهاقتِهم في الضّيعة.","html":"<p>On preserving the name unchanged, al-Intiṣāf says: “This indicates that the nominative precedes the other case forms. They preserve its familiar shape; the name became known thus. His name ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā was avoided in favour of his kunya because it was disliked.”</p><p>Falīta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَلِيتة</bdi>) has fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>); Fukayta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فُكَيْتة</bdi>) with kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) and the diminutive is also transmitted. The report that the Messenger named al-Muhallab’s father Abū Ṣufra is not mentioned in Jāmiʿ al-uṣūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأصول</bdi>). Al-Muhallab is Abū Saʿīd al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufra; his father’s name was Ẓālim ibn Surrāq ibn Ṣubayḥ al-Azdī. Al-Muhallab fought the celebrated wars against the Khārijites and died in 83 at Marw al-Rūdh under ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān. He belongs to the first generation of Basran Successors and saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.</p><p>“It is also said he was so named” subdivides the third explanation; it is not a fourth. The kunya is preferred either because it uniquely identifies him and his name would confuse; or both identify him equally and the kunya was chosen though the name was possible; or for a subtle point: to signify that he belongs to Hell, either simply or with mockery. The author of al-Miftāḥ indicates the first explanation and the first version of the third.</p><p>Ibn Kathīr reads Lahb with quiescent hāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ه</bdi>); the others with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). Abū l-Baqāʾ calls them two dialectal forms. Mā’s accusative position is as an absolute object: “What benefit?” Abū l-Baqāʾ gives the two interpretations and says it cannot mean “that which.” The author is reported to have said: māl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مال</bdi>) is general; Bedouins use it of camels, while their landed notables use it of an estate.</p>","lemma":"لئلا يغير منه شئ فيشكل على السامع","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"111:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ تَبَّتْ يَدَآ أَبِى لَهَبٍ وَتَبَّ","en":"The power of Abu Lahab will perish, and he will perish"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ تَبَّتْ يَدَآ أَبِى لَهَبٍ وَتَبَّ ﴿1﴾","quran_en":"1. The power of Abu Lahab will perish, and he will perish"},{"id":"K111.03","q":"111:2","s":111,"surah":"al-Masad","surah_ar":"المسد","title":"al-Masad · Wealth and earnings","cairo":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","source_page_first":813,"source_page_last":816,"chunk":"passages-111","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Wealth and earnings [IV:814–815]","html":"<p>Mā aghnā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أغنى</bdi>) is either an interrogative expressing denial, with mā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ما</bdi>) accusative, or a negation. Wa-mā kasaba is nominative; mā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ما</bdi>) is either relative, “what he earned,” or converts the clause into a verbal noun, “his earning.” His wealth and what he earned through it did not benefit him: capital and profits; or livestock and their offspring and benefits—he owned abundant breeding stock; or inherited wealth and what he acquired himself; or long-held and newly acquired property.</p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās explains “what he earned” as his children. Abū Lahab’s sons are said to have brought a dispute before him and begun fighting. He rose to separate them; one pushed him and he fell. Angry, he said, “Take this foul earning away from me!” Hence the Prophet’s saying, “The best of what a man eats is from his earning, and his child is from his earning.” Al-Ḍaḥḥāk explains it as his wealth and evil action, meaning his schemes of hostility against the Messenger. Qatāda takes it as the deeds he thought amounted to something, as in “We turned to whatever deeds they had done” [25:23]. It is related that he said, “If my nephew’s claims are true, I shall ransom myself from it with my wealth and children.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K111.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Livestock, earnings, and the reading [XVI:627]","html":"<p>On sābiyāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سابياء</bdi>) [the transcription also writes sāyibāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سايباء</bdi>)], al-Nihāya says: “The offspring and abundance of livestock; one says a household has sābiyāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سابياء</bdi>), plural sawābī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سوابي</bdi>). Originally it is the membrane from which the newborn emerges, or the afterbirth.” Some say it is a diptote naming the offspring. Tālid means long-held wealth, opposite to newly acquired ṭārif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">طارف</bdi>). Abū Dāwūd transmitted “the best of what a man eats” from ʿĀʾisha. The reading sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>) with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) is the well-known one; the reading with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) is noncanonical.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ما أغنى استفهام في معنى الإنكار، ومحله النصب أو نفى وما كسب مرفوع. وما موصولة أو مصدرية بمعنى: ومكسوبه. أو: وكسبه. والمعنى: لم ينفعه ماله وما كسب بماله، يعنى: رأس المال والأرباح. أو ماشيته وما كسب من نسلها ومنافعها، وكان ذا سابياء. أو ماله الذي ورثه من أبيه والذي كسبه بنفسه. أو ماله التالد والطارف. وعن ابن عباس: ما كسب ولده. وحكى أن بنى أبى لهب احتكموا إليه، فاقتتلوا، فقام يحجز بينهم، فدفعه بعضهم فوقع، فغضب، فقال: أخرجوا عنى الكسب الخبيث: ومنه قوله عليه السلام «إن أطيب ما يأكل الرجل من كسبه وإن ولده من كسبه» وعن الضحاك: ما ينفعه ماله وعمله الخبيث، يعنى كيده في عداوة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم. وعن قتادة: عمله الذي ظن أنه منه على شيء، كقوله وقدمنا إلى ما عملوا من عمل وروى أنه كان يقول: إن كان ما يقول ابن أخى حقا فأنا أفتدى منه نفسي بمالي وولدى\n\nقولُه: (وكانَ ذا سايباء)، النهاية: «السَّايباء: النتاج في المواشي وكثرتِها، يقالُ: إنّ لآلِ فلانٍ سايباء، والجمع السَّوابي، وهي في الأصل الجلدةُ التي يخرجُ منها الولد، وقيل: هي المشيمة». وعن بعضهم: سايباء غيرُ منصرف، وهو اسمُ النتاج.\n\nقولُه: (التَّالِد)، وهو المالُ القديم، نقيضُ الطارف.\n\nقولُه: (إن أطيبَ ما يأكلُ الرجل)، الحديثُ أخرجه أبو داودَ، عن عائشة-رضي الله عنها.\n\nقولُه: (سَيَصْلى: قرئَ بفتحِ الياء)، وهي المشهورة، وبالضمّ شاذّة.","layers":[{"id":"K111.03","heading":"K111.03 — Wealth and earnings [IV:814–815]","text":"Mā aghnā (أغنى) is either an interrogative expressing denial, with mā (ما) accusative, or a negation. Wa-mā kasaba is nominative; mā (ما) is either relative, “what he earned,” or converts the clause into a verbal noun, “his earning.” His wealth and what he earned through it did not benefit him: capital and profits; or livestock and their offspring and benefits—he owned abundant breeding stock; or inherited wealth and what he acquired himself; or long-held and newly acquired property.\n\nIbn ʿAbbās explains “what he earned” as his children. Abū Lahab’s sons are said to have brought a dispute before him and begun fighting. He rose to separate them; one pushed him and he fell. Angry, he said, “Take this foul earning away from me!” Hence the Prophet’s saying, “The best of what a man eats is from his earning, and his child is from his earning.” Al-Ḍaḥḥāk explains it as his wealth and evil action, meaning his schemes of hostility against the Messenger. Qatāda takes it as the deeds he thought amounted to something, as in “We turned to whatever deeds they had done” [25:23]. It is related that he said, “If my nephew’s claims are true, I shall ransom myself from it with my wealth and children.”","title":"Wealth and earnings [IV:814–815]","author":"kashshaf","surah":111,"ar":"ما أغنى استفهام في معنى الإنكار، ومحله النصب أو نفى وما كسب مرفوع. وما موصولة أو مصدرية بمعنى: ومكسوبه. أو: وكسبه. والمعنى: لم ينفعه ماله وما كسب بماله، يعنى: رأس المال والأرباح. أو ماشيته وما كسب من نسلها ومنافعها، وكان ذا سابياء. أو ماله الذي ورثه من أبيه والذي كسبه بنفسه. أو ماله التالد والطارف. وعن ابن عباس: ما كسب ولده. وحكى أن بنى أبى لهب احتكموا إليه، فاقتتلوا، فقام يحجز بينهم، فدفعه بعضهم فوقع، فغضب، فقال: أخرجوا عنى الكسب الخبيث: ومنه قوله عليه السلام «إن أطيب ما يأكل الرجل من كسبه وإن ولده من كسبه» وعن الضحاك: ما ينفعه ماله وعمله الخبيث، يعنى كيده في عداوة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم. وعن قتادة: عمله الذي ظن أنه منه على شيء، كقوله وقدمنا إلى ما عملوا من عمل وروى أنه كان يقول: إن كان ما يقول ابن أخى حقا فأنا أفتدى منه نفسي بمالي وولدى","html":"<p>Mā aghnā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أغنى</bdi>) is either an interrogative expressing denial, with mā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ما</bdi>) accusative, or a negation. Wa-mā kasaba is nominative; mā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ما</bdi>) is either relative, “what he earned,” or converts the clause into a verbal noun, “his earning.” His wealth and what he earned through it did not benefit him: capital and profits; or livestock and their offspring and benefits—he owned abundant breeding stock; or inherited wealth and what he acquired himself; or long-held and newly acquired property.</p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās explains “what he earned” as his children. Abū Lahab’s sons are said to have brought a dispute before him and begun fighting. He rose to separate them; one pushed him and he fell. Angry, he said, “Take this foul earning away from me!” Hence the Prophet’s saying, “The best of what a man eats is from his earning, and his child is from his earning.” Al-Ḍaḥḥāk explains it as his wealth and evil action, meaning his schemes of hostility against the Messenger. Qatāda takes it as the deeds he thought amounted to something, as in “We turned to whatever deeds they had done” [25:23]. It is related that he said, “If my nephew’s claims are true, I shall ransom myself from it with my wealth and children.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.05","heading":"T111.05 — Livestock, earnings, and the reading [XVI:627]","text":"On sābiyāʾ (سابياء) [the transcription also writes sāyibāʾ (سايباء)], al-Nihāya says: “The offspring and abundance of livestock; one says a household has sābiyāʾ (سابياء), plural sawābī (سوابي). Originally it is the membrane from which the newborn emerges, or the afterbirth.” Some say it is a diptote naming the offspring. Tālid means long-held wealth, opposite to newly acquired ṭārif (طارف). Abū Dāwūd transmitted “the best of what a man eats” from ʿĀʾisha. The reading sa-yaṣlā (سيصلى) with fatḥa (فَتْحة) is the well-known one; the reading with ḍamma (ضَمّة) is noncanonical.","title":"Livestock, earnings, and the reading [XVI:627]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.03"],"anchor":"K111.03","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (وكانَ ذا سايباء)، النهاية: «السَّايباء: النتاج في المواشي وكثرتِها، يقالُ: إنّ لآلِ فلانٍ سايباء، والجمع السَّوابي، وهي في الأصل الجلدةُ التي يخرجُ منها الولد، وقيل: هي المشيمة». وعن بعضهم: سايباء غيرُ منصرف، وهو اسمُ النتاج.\n\nقولُه: (التَّالِد)، وهو المالُ القديم، نقيضُ الطارف.\n\nقولُه: (إن أطيبَ ما يأكلُ الرجل)، الحديثُ أخرجه أبو داودَ، عن عائشة-رضي الله عنها.\n\nقولُه: (سَيَصْلى: قرئَ بفتحِ الياء)، وهي المشهورة، وبالضمّ شاذّة.","html":"<p>On sābiyāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سابياء</bdi>) [the transcription also writes sāyibāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سايباء</bdi>)], al-Nihāya says: “The offspring and abundance of livestock; one says a household has sābiyāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سابياء</bdi>), plural sawābī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سوابي</bdi>). Originally it is the membrane from which the newborn emerges, or the afterbirth.” Some say it is a diptote naming the offspring. Tālid means long-held wealth, opposite to newly acquired ṭārif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">طارف</bdi>). Abū Dāwūd transmitted “the best of what a man eats” from ʿĀʾisha. The reading sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>) with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) is the well-known one; the reading with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) is noncanonical.</p>","lemma":"وكان ذا سايباء","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"111:2","number":2,"ar":"مَآ أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥ وَمَا كَسَبَ","en":"His wealth and gains will not exempt him"}],"quran_ar":"مَآ أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥ وَمَا كَسَبَ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"2. His wealth and gains will not exempt him"},{"id":"K111.04","q":"111:3–4","s":111,"surah":"al-Masad","surah_ar":"المسد","title":"al-Masad · His wife and the firewood","cairo":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","source_page_first":813,"source_page_last":816,"chunk":"passages-111","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"His wife and the firewood [IV:815]","html":"<p>Sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>) is read with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on yāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ي</bdi>) and also with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>), in both lightened and doubled forms. Sīn expresses threat: it will inevitably occur, however delayed. His wife is Umm Jamīl daughter of Ḥarb, Abū Sufyān’s sister. She carried bundles of thorns, prickles and saʿdān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سعدان</bdi>) and scattered them at night on the Messenger’s path. Or she went about carrying tales. A malicious gossip who corrupts relations is said to “carry firewood among them”: he kindles their hostility and causes evil. The poet says:\n“A fair woman never found upon a blameworthy course,\nnor walking among the tribe with damp firewood.”\nThe wood is called damp to suggest smoke, an additional evil.</p><p>“His wife” is nominative, conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>): he and his wife will burn; “about her neck” then expresses a circumstance. Or “his wife” is the subject and “about her neck” its predicate. “Carrier of firewood” is also read accusative as abuse. I prefer this reading: whoever loves reviling Umm Jamīl has approached the Messenger through something fine (jamīl). Ḥammālatun li-l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">للحطب</bdi>) and ḥammālatan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالةً</bdi>) li-l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">للحطب</bdi>) are also read, with tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>) and nominative or accusative. Wa-murayʾatuhu, “and his little wife,” is read as a diminutive.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K111.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The woman and damp wood [XVI:628]","html":"<p>“Never hunted” means never found: she is likened to an oryx and its description applied to her. Lāʾima means something blameworthy: she was never found pursuing a blameworthy quality. The poet praises her honour. Al-khaṭar al-raṭb is also transmitted: damp wood used to make an enclosure. The meaning is that she did not go among people carrying tales and instilling hostility.</p><p>On smoke as an additional evil: he did not merely make it wood, but damp wood, an intensifying completion (īghāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيغال</bdi>) and tatmīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تتميم</bdi>)) for hyperbole. Imruʾ al-Qays says:\n“I bore a Rudaynī spear whose point was like\nthe brilliance of a flame unmixed with smoke.”\nʿĀṣim reads ḥammālata l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالةَ الحطب</bdi>) accusative; the others nominative.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.06"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"سيصلى قرئ بفتح الياء وبضمها: مخففا ومشددا، والسين للوعيد، أى: هو كائن لا محالة وإن تراخى وقته وامرأته هي أم جميل بنت حرب أخت أبى سفيان، وكانت تحمل حزمة من الشوك والحسك والسعدان فتنثرها بالليل في طريق رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم. وقيل: كانت تمشى بالنميمة: ويقال للمشاء بالنمائم المفسد بين الناس: يحمل الحطب بينهم، أى: يوقد بينهم النائرة ويورث الشر. قال: من البيض لم تصطد على ظهر لأمة... ولم تمش بين الحى بالحطب الرطب جعله رطبا ليدل على التدخين الذي هو زيادة في الشر، ورفعت عطفا على الضمير في سيصلى أى: سيصلى هو وامرأته. وفي جيدها في موضع الحال. أو على الابتداء، وفي جيدها: الخبر. وقرئ: حمالة الحطب، بالنصب على الشتم، وأنا أستحب هذه القراءة، وقد توسل إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بجميل: من أحب شتم أم جميل. وقرئ: حمالة للحطب. وحمالة للحطب: بالتنوين، والرفع والنصب. وقرئ: ومريته بالتصغير.\n\nقولُه: (مِن البِيضِ لم تُصْطَدْ) البيت، لم تُصْطَدْ: لم توجَد؛ شُبّهتْ بالمها وأُجري صفتُها عليها. واللامةُ: الأمرُ الذي يُلام عليه، أي: لم توجدْ راكبةَ خصلةٍ تُلامُ عليها؛ يصفُ امرأةً بكرامةِ العِرْض. ويُروى: بالخطرِ الرَّطب. الخطرُ الرطبُ: [الحطبُ] الذي يُخْطر به، أي: يُجعلْ منه [حظيرةٌ]، والمعنى: لم يمشِ بالنميمةِ بين الناسِ، فتُلقى فيهم العداوة.\n\nقولُه: (جعلُه رطبًا ليدلَّ على التدخينِ الذي هو زيادةٌ في الشر)، يعني: ما كفى بأن جعلَه [حطبًا] بل جعلَه رطبًا للإيغالِ [والتتميمِ] لإرادةِ المبالغة، قال امرؤ القيس:\n\nحملتُ رُدَيْنيًّا كأنّ سنانَه سَنا لهَب لم يتصلْ بدُخان\n\nقولُه: (قُرئَ: ﴿حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ﴾، بالنصب)، عاصمٌ، والباقون: بالرفع.","layers":[{"id":"K111.04","heading":"K111.04 — His wife and the firewood [IV:815]","text":"Sa-yaṣlā (لا) is read with fatḥa (فَتْحة) on yāʾ (ي) and also with ḍamma (ضَمّة), in both lightened and doubled forms. Sīn expresses threat: it will inevitably occur, however delayed. His wife is Umm Jamīl daughter of Ḥarb, Abū Sufyān’s sister. She carried bundles of thorns, prickles and saʿdān (سعدان) and scattered them at night on the Messenger’s path. Or she went about carrying tales. A malicious gossip who corrupts relations is said to “carry firewood among them”: he kindles their hostility and causes evil. The poet says:\n“A fair woman never found upon a blameworthy course,\nnor walking among the tribe with damp firewood.”\nThe wood is called damp to suggest smoke, an additional evil.\n\n“His wife” is nominative, conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (سيصلى): he and his wife will burn; “about her neck” then expresses a circumstance. Or “his wife” is the subject and “about her neck” its predicate. “Carrier of firewood” is also read accusative as abuse. I prefer this reading: whoever loves reviling Umm Jamīl has approached the Messenger through something fine (jamīl). Ḥammālatun li-l-ḥaṭab (للحطب) and ḥammālatan (حمّالةً) li-l-ḥaṭab (للحطب) are also read, with tanwīn (تنوين) and nominative or accusative. Wa-murayʾatuhu, “and his little wife,” is read as a diminutive.","title":"His wife and the firewood [IV:815]","author":"kashshaf","surah":111,"ar":"سيصلى قرئ بفتح الياء وبضمها: مخففا ومشددا، والسين للوعيد، أى: هو كائن لا محالة وإن تراخى وقته وامرأته هي أم جميل بنت حرب أخت أبى سفيان، وكانت تحمل حزمة من الشوك والحسك والسعدان فتنثرها بالليل في طريق رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم. وقيل: كانت تمشى بالنميمة: ويقال للمشاء بالنمائم المفسد بين الناس: يحمل الحطب بينهم، أى: يوقد بينهم النائرة ويورث الشر. قال: من البيض لم تصطد على ظهر لأمة... ولم تمش بين الحى بالحطب الرطب جعله رطبا ليدل على التدخين الذي هو زيادة في الشر، ورفعت عطفا على الضمير في سيصلى أى: سيصلى هو وامرأته. وفي جيدها في موضع الحال. أو على الابتداء، وفي جيدها: الخبر. وقرئ: حمالة الحطب، بالنصب على الشتم، وأنا أستحب هذه القراءة، وقد توسل إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بجميل: من أحب شتم أم جميل. وقرئ: حمالة للحطب. وحمالة للحطب: بالتنوين، والرفع والنصب. وقرئ: ومريته بالتصغير.","html":"<p>Sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>) is read with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on yāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ي</bdi>) and also with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>), in both lightened and doubled forms. Sīn expresses threat: it will inevitably occur, however delayed. His wife is Umm Jamīl daughter of Ḥarb, Abū Sufyān’s sister. She carried bundles of thorns, prickles and saʿdān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سعدان</bdi>) and scattered them at night on the Messenger’s path. Or she went about carrying tales. A malicious gossip who corrupts relations is said to “carry firewood among them”: he kindles their hostility and causes evil. The poet says:\n“A fair woman never found upon a blameworthy course,\nnor walking among the tribe with damp firewood.”\nThe wood is called damp to suggest smoke, an additional evil.</p><p>“His wife” is nominative, conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>): he and his wife will burn; “about her neck” then expresses a circumstance. Or “his wife” is the subject and “about her neck” its predicate. “Carrier of firewood” is also read accusative as abuse. I prefer this reading: whoever loves reviling Umm Jamīl has approached the Messenger through something fine (jamīl). Ḥammālatun li-l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">للحطب</bdi>) and ḥammālatan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالةً</bdi>) li-l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">للحطب</bdi>) are also read, with tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>) and nominative or accusative. Wa-murayʾatuhu, “and his little wife,” is read as a diminutive.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.06","heading":"T111.06 — The woman and damp wood [XVI:628]","text":"“Never hunted” means never found: she is likened to an oryx and its description applied to her. Lāʾima means something blameworthy: she was never found pursuing a blameworthy quality. The poet praises her honour. Al-khaṭar al-raṭb is also transmitted: damp wood used to make an enclosure. The meaning is that she did not go among people carrying tales and instilling hostility.\n\nOn smoke as an additional evil: he did not merely make it wood, but damp wood, an intensifying completion (īghāl (إيغال) and tatmīm (تتميم)) for hyperbole. Imruʾ al-Qays says:\n“I bore a Rudaynī spear whose point was like\nthe brilliance of a flame unmixed with smoke.”\nʿĀṣim reads ḥammālata l-ḥaṭab (حمّالةَ الحطب) accusative; the others nominative.","title":"The woman and damp wood [XVI:628]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.04"],"anchor":"K111.04","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (مِن البِيضِ لم تُصْطَدْ) البيت، لم تُصْطَدْ: لم توجَد؛ شُبّهتْ بالمها وأُجري صفتُها عليها. واللامةُ: الأمرُ الذي يُلام عليه، أي: لم توجدْ راكبةَ خصلةٍ تُلامُ عليها؛ يصفُ امرأةً بكرامةِ العِرْض. ويُروى: بالخطرِ الرَّطب. الخطرُ الرطبُ: [الحطبُ] الذي يُخْطر به، أي: يُجعلْ منه [حظيرةٌ]، والمعنى: لم يمشِ بالنميمةِ بين الناسِ، فتُلقى فيهم العداوة.\n\nقولُه: (جعلُه رطبًا ليدلَّ على التدخينِ الذي هو زيادةٌ في الشر)، يعني: ما كفى بأن جعلَه [حطبًا] بل جعلَه رطبًا للإيغالِ [والتتميمِ] لإرادةِ المبالغة، قال امرؤ القيس:\n\nحملتُ رُدَيْنيًّا كأنّ سنانَه سَنا لهَب لم يتصلْ بدُخان\n\nقولُه: (قُرئَ: ﴿حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ﴾، بالنصب)، عاصمٌ، والباقون: بالرفع.","html":"<p>“Never hunted” means never found: she is likened to an oryx and its description applied to her. Lāʾima means something blameworthy: she was never found pursuing a blameworthy quality. The poet praises her honour. Al-khaṭar al-raṭb is also transmitted: damp wood used to make an enclosure. The meaning is that she did not go among people carrying tales and instilling hostility.</p><p>On smoke as an additional evil: he did not merely make it wood, but damp wood, an intensifying completion (īghāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيغال</bdi>) and tatmīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تتميم</bdi>)) for hyperbole. Imruʾ al-Qays says:\n“I bore a Rudaynī spear whose point was like\nthe brilliance of a flame unmixed with smoke.”\nʿĀṣim reads ḥammālata l-ḥaṭab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالةَ الحطب</bdi>) accusative; the others nominative.</p>","lemma":"من البيض لم تصطد","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"111:3","number":3,"ar":"سَيَصْلَىٰ نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ","en":"He will be plunged in flaming Fire"},{"key":"111:4","number":4,"ar":"وَٱمْرَأَتُهُۥ حَمَّالَةَ ٱلْحَطَبِ","en":"And his wife, the wood-carrier"}],"quran_ar":"سَيَصْلَىٰ نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ ﴿3﴾ وَٱمْرَأَتُهُۥ حَمَّالَةَ ٱلْحَطَبِ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"3. He will be plunged in flaming Fire 4. And his wife, the wood-carrier"},{"id":"K111.05","q":"111:5","s":111,"surah":"al-Masad","surah_ar":"المسد","title":"al-Masad · The rope, the taunt, and punishment","cairo":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","source_page_first":813,"source_page_last":816,"chunk":"passages-111","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The rope, the taunt, and punishment [IV:815–817]","html":"<p>Masad is tightly twisted cord, whether palm fibre, leather, or another material. The poet says, “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels.” A man mamsūd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ممسود</bdi>) in build is firmly knit. Thus she has a twisted rope about her neck, carrying that bundle of thorns and fastening it about her neck, as wood-gatherers do. This debases and belittles her, depicting her as one of the serving-women who gather wood, to anger both her and her husband despite their house of honour and their wealth and prosperity.</p><p>Someone taunted al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿUtba ibn Abī Lahab with “the carrier of firewood.” He replied:\n“Why have you turned to insulting and diminishing me?\nWhat reproach is there in the carrier of firewood?\nA radiant woman, whose broad blaze shone in glory,\noffspring of an elder of profound ancestral distinction.”</p><p>Or the meaning is that in Hell she will appear as she did when carrying the thorn-bundle: always bearing a bundle of hellfire wood from zaqqūm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زقّوم</bdi>) or ḍarīʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضريع</bdi>), with a rope twisted from the Fire’s chains about her neck. Every criminal is punished in a manner corresponding to his crime.</p><p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat Tabbat, I hope God will not place him and Abū Lahab together in one abode.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K111.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The twisted camel-hide rope [XVI:629]","html":"<p>The verse “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels” continues, according to al-Zajjāj:\n“Reddish-white, choice, with marrow approaching maturity.”\nAl-Muṭliʿ has “neither old she-camels nor young ones of the ḥiqqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حِقّة</bdi>) age.” Umirra means twisted. Ayāniq is the plural of aynuq, itself a plural of nāqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناقة</bdi>): the cord is made from their hides. Ṣuhb qualifies ayāniq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أيانق</bdi>). An aṣhab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أصهب</bdi>) camel has white mingled with red. Rāhiq is borrowed from a boy approaching maturity. Anyāb is the plural of nāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناب</bdi>) [an old she-camel]. The rope is made neither from a very young nor an old animal’s hide, but from a strong young adult’s.</p><p>On “firmly knit,” al-Jawharī gives “a girl of well-knit build.” Mawāhin is plural of māhina (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ماهنة</bdi>): mahna, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>), is service; māhin (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ماهن</bdi>) is a servant.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.07"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Anger, the poem, and final syntax [XVI:630–631]","html":"<p>Maʿiḍtu and imtaʿaḍtu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">امتعضت</bdi>) mean I became angry and found something distressing. In “what did you intend toward insulting me,” aradta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أردت</bdi>) includes the meaning of turning toward, and therefore takes ilā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلى</bdi>). A shādikha (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">شادخة</bdi>) is a horse’s broad white blaze extending from forelock to nose without touching the eyes, a feature of noble horses. “The elder” is taken as ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib—but this is not correct, since she is Ḥarb’s daughter and Abū Sufyān’s sister, as stated.</p><p>On the interpretation that her appearance in Hell matches her former appearance: “and his wife, carrier of firewood” is then a circumstantial clause referring to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>), or “his wife” is conjoined to that pronoun. On the earlier interpretation it cannot be a circumstantial clause, but one sentence is conjoined to another. Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Imraʾatuhu has two analyses: a subject with ḥammāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالة</bdi>) as predicate; or conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>). On the latter, ḥammāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالة</bdi>) is either an adjective for the preceding noun or stands for ‘while she is a carrier.’”</p><p>End of the sūrah.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T111.08"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"المسد: الذي فتل من الحبال فتلا شديدا، من ليف كان أو جلد، أو غيرهما. قال: ومسد أمر من أيانق ورجل ممسود الخلق مجدوله. والمعنى: في جيدها حبل مما مسد من الحبال، وأنها تحمل تلك الحزمة من الشوك وتربطها في جيدها كما يفعل الحطابون: تخسيسا لحالها، وتحقيرا لها، وتصويرا لها بصورة بعض الحطابات من المواهن، لتمتعض من ذلك ويمتعض بعلها، وهما في بيت العز والشرف. وفي منصب الثروة والجدة. ولقد عير بعض الناس الفضل بن العباس ابن عتبة ابن أبى لهب بحمالة الحطب، فقال: ماذا أردت إلى شتمي ومنقصتي... أم ما تعير من حمالة الحطب غراء شادخة في المجد غرتها... كانت سليلة شيخ ناقب الحسب ويحتمل أن يكون المعنى: أن حالها تكون في نار جهنم على الصورة التي كانت عليها حين كانت تحمل حزمة الشوك، فلا تزال على ظهرها حزمة من حطب النار من شجرة الزقوم أو من الضريع، وفي جيدها حبل من ما مسد من سلاسل النار: كما يعذب كل مجرم بما يجانس حاله في جرمه. عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة تبت رجوت أن لا يجمع الله بينه وبين أبى لهب في دار واحدة ».\n\nقولُه: (ومَسَدٍ أُمِرَّ مِن أيانق)، تمامُه عن الزجاج.\n\nصُهْبٍ عِتاقٍ ذاتِ مخٌّ راهقِ\n\nالأصهب، وفي \"المطلع\": ليسَ بأنيابٍ ولا حقائق. أُمِرّ: أَيْ فُتِل. الأيانقُ جمعُ أَيْنَق، وهو جمعُ ناقة؛ أرادَ أن المسدَ فُتِلَ من جلدِ الأيانق. صُهبٍ: صفةٌ لأيانق. الأصهبُ من الإبل: الذي يخالطُ بياضَه حمرة. راهق: مستعارٌ من راهقَ الغلامُ فهو مراهق. والأنيابُ جمعُ ناب. يعني: هذا المسَد لم يُتَخذ من جلدِ صغيرةٍ ولا كبيرة، وإنما اتخذ من جلدِ فتيّةٍ قويّة.\n\nقولُه: (مجدولُه)، الجوهري: \" جاريةٌ مجدولةُ الخَلْق: حسنةُ الجدل\".\n\nقولُه: (من المواهِنِ)، جمعُ الماهنة، الَمهْنَةُ بالفتحِ: الخدمة، والماهَنُ: الخادم.\n\nقولُه: (لِتَمْتعض)، مَعِضْتُ من ذلك الأمرِ أَمعضُ معضًا، وامتعضتُ منه، إذا غضبتَ وشقَّ عليك.\n\nقولُه: (ماذا أَرَدْتَ) البيتين، أَرَدْتَ: أي: مِلْتَ: ضُمّنَ الإرادةُ معنى الميل وعُدِّي بإلى. الشَّادخة: الغُرَّةُ التي فَشَتْ في الوجهِ من الناصيةِ إلى الأنفِ ولم تُصبْ العينين، يوصفُ بها كرائمُ الخيل. والمرادُ بالشيخِ عبدُ المطلبِ وليسَ به؛ لأنها بنتُ حربٍ، أُختُ أبي سفيانَ كما ذكره.\n\nقولُه: (ويُحتملُ أن يكونَ المعنى أن حالها تكونُ في نارِ جهنّمَ على الصورةِ التي كانت عليها)، فعلى هذا: ﴿وَامْرَأَتُهُ حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ﴾، الجملةُ حالٌ من الضميرِ في ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾، أو يعطفُ ﴿وَامْرَأَتُهُ﴾ على الضمير. وعلى الأولِ لا يجوزُ الحال، بل عطفُ جملةٍ على جملة، قالَ أبو البقاء: «(امرأتُه) فيه وجهان: أحدهما مبتدأٌ والخبرُ حَمّالة\"، وثانيهما هو معطوفٌ على الضمير في ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾؛ فعلى هذا، في \"حَمّالة\" وجهان: أحدهما نعتٌ لِما قبلَه، والثاني تقديرُه: وهي حَمّالة».\n\nتَمَّتِ السُّورة","layers":[{"id":"K111.05","heading":"K111.05 — The rope, the taunt, and punishment [IV:815–817]","text":"Masad is tightly twisted cord, whether palm fibre, leather, or another material. The poet says, “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels.” A man mamsūd (ممسود) in build is firmly knit. Thus she has a twisted rope about her neck, carrying that bundle of thorns and fastening it about her neck, as wood-gatherers do. This debases and belittles her, depicting her as one of the serving-women who gather wood, to anger both her and her husband despite their house of honour and their wealth and prosperity.\n\nSomeone taunted al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿUtba ibn Abī Lahab with “the carrier of firewood.” He replied:\n“Why have you turned to insulting and diminishing me?\nWhat reproach is there in the carrier of firewood?\nA radiant woman, whose broad blaze shone in glory,\noffspring of an elder of profound ancestral distinction.”\n\nOr the meaning is that in Hell she will appear as she did when carrying the thorn-bundle: always bearing a bundle of hellfire wood from zaqqūm (زقّوم) or ḍarīʿ (ضريع), with a rope twisted from the Fire’s chains about her neck. Every criminal is punished in a manner corresponding to his crime.\n\nFrom God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat Tabbat, I hope God will not place him and Abū Lahab together in one abode.”","title":"The rope, the taunt, and punishment [IV:815–817]","author":"kashshaf","surah":111,"ar":"المسد: الذي فتل من الحبال فتلا شديدا، من ليف كان أو جلد، أو غيرهما. قال: ومسد أمر من أيانق ورجل ممسود الخلق مجدوله. والمعنى: في جيدها حبل مما مسد من الحبال، وأنها تحمل تلك الحزمة من الشوك وتربطها في جيدها كما يفعل الحطابون: تخسيسا لحالها، وتحقيرا لها، وتصويرا لها بصورة بعض الحطابات من المواهن، لتمتعض من ذلك ويمتعض بعلها، وهما في بيت العز والشرف. وفي منصب الثروة والجدة. ولقد عير بعض الناس الفضل بن العباس ابن عتبة ابن أبى لهب بحمالة الحطب، فقال: ماذا أردت إلى شتمي ومنقصتي... أم ما تعير من حمالة الحطب غراء شادخة في المجد غرتها... كانت سليلة شيخ ناقب الحسب ويحتمل أن يكون المعنى: أن حالها تكون في نار جهنم على الصورة التي كانت عليها حين كانت تحمل حزمة الشوك، فلا تزال على ظهرها حزمة من حطب النار من شجرة الزقوم أو من الضريع، وفي جيدها حبل من ما مسد من سلاسل النار: كما يعذب كل مجرم بما يجانس حاله في جرمه. عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة تبت رجوت أن لا يجمع الله بينه وبين أبى لهب في دار واحدة ».","html":"<p>Masad is tightly twisted cord, whether palm fibre, leather, or another material. The poet says, “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels.” A man mamsūd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ممسود</bdi>) in build is firmly knit. Thus she has a twisted rope about her neck, carrying that bundle of thorns and fastening it about her neck, as wood-gatherers do. This debases and belittles her, depicting her as one of the serving-women who gather wood, to anger both her and her husband despite their house of honour and their wealth and prosperity.</p><p>Someone taunted al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿUtba ibn Abī Lahab with “the carrier of firewood.” He replied:\n“Why have you turned to insulting and diminishing me?\nWhat reproach is there in the carrier of firewood?\nA radiant woman, whose broad blaze shone in glory,\noffspring of an elder of profound ancestral distinction.”</p><p>Or the meaning is that in Hell she will appear as she did when carrying the thorn-bundle: always bearing a bundle of hellfire wood from zaqqūm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زقّوم</bdi>) or ḍarīʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضريع</bdi>), with a rope twisted from the Fire’s chains about her neck. Every criminal is punished in a manner corresponding to his crime.</p><p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites Sūrat Tabbat, I hope God will not place him and Abū Lahab together in one abode.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.07","heading":"T111.07 — The twisted camel-hide rope [XVI:629]","text":"The verse “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels” continues, according to al-Zajjāj:\n“Reddish-white, choice, with marrow approaching maturity.”\nAl-Muṭliʿ has “neither old she-camels nor young ones of the ḥiqqa (حِقّة) age.” Umirra means twisted. Ayāniq is the plural of aynuq, itself a plural of nāqa (ناقة): the cord is made from their hides. Ṣuhb qualifies ayāniq (أيانق). An aṣhab (أصهب) camel has white mingled with red. Rāhiq is borrowed from a boy approaching maturity. Anyāb is the plural of nāb (ناب) [an old she-camel]. The rope is made neither from a very young nor an old animal’s hide, but from a strong young adult’s.\n\nOn “firmly knit,” al-Jawharī gives “a girl of well-knit build.” Mawāhin is plural of māhina (ماهنة): mahna, with fatḥa (فَتْحة) on mīm (م), is service; māhin (ماهن) is a servant.","title":"The twisted camel-hide rope [XVI:629]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.05"],"anchor":"K111.05","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (ومَسَدٍ أُمِرَّ مِن أيانق)، تمامُه عن الزجاج.\n\nصُهْبٍ عِتاقٍ ذاتِ مخٌّ راهقِ\n\nالأصهب، وفي \"المطلع\": ليسَ بأنيابٍ ولا حقائق. أُمِرّ: أَيْ فُتِل. الأيانقُ جمعُ أَيْنَق، وهو جمعُ ناقة؛ أرادَ أن المسدَ فُتِلَ من جلدِ الأيانق. صُهبٍ: صفةٌ لأيانق. الأصهبُ من الإبل: الذي يخالطُ بياضَه حمرة. راهق: مستعارٌ من راهقَ الغلامُ فهو مراهق. والأنيابُ جمعُ ناب. يعني: هذا المسَد لم يُتَخذ من جلدِ صغيرةٍ ولا كبيرة، وإنما اتخذ من جلدِ فتيّةٍ قويّة.\n\nقولُه: (مجدولُه)، الجوهري: \" جاريةٌ مجدولةُ الخَلْق: حسنةُ الجدل\".\n\nقولُه: (من المواهِنِ)، جمعُ الماهنة، الَمهْنَةُ بالفتحِ: الخدمة، والماهَنُ: الخادم.","html":"<p>The verse “And a rope tightly twisted from she-camels” continues, according to al-Zajjāj:\n“Reddish-white, choice, with marrow approaching maturity.”\nAl-Muṭliʿ has “neither old she-camels nor young ones of the ḥiqqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حِقّة</bdi>) age.” Umirra means twisted. Ayāniq is the plural of aynuq, itself a plural of nāqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناقة</bdi>): the cord is made from their hides. Ṣuhb qualifies ayāniq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أيانق</bdi>). An aṣhab (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أصهب</bdi>) camel has white mingled with red. Rāhiq is borrowed from a boy approaching maturity. Anyāb is the plural of nāb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناب</bdi>) [an old she-camel]. The rope is made neither from a very young nor an old animal’s hide, but from a strong young adult’s.</p><p>On “firmly knit,” al-Jawharī gives “a girl of well-knit build.” Mawāhin is plural of māhina (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ماهنة</bdi>): mahna, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) on mīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">م</bdi>), is service; māhin (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ماهن</bdi>) is a servant.</p>","lemma":"ومسد أمر من أيانق","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T111.08","heading":"T111.08 — Anger, the poem, and final syntax [XVI:630–631]","text":"Maʿiḍtu and imtaʿaḍtu (امتعضت) mean I became angry and found something distressing. In “what did you intend toward insulting me,” aradta (أردت) includes the meaning of turning toward, and therefore takes ilā (إلى). A shādikha (شادخة) is a horse’s broad white blaze extending from forelock to nose without touching the eyes, a feature of noble horses. “The elder” is taken as ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib—but this is not correct, since she is Ḥarb’s daughter and Abū Sufyān’s sister, as stated.\n\nOn the interpretation that her appearance in Hell matches her former appearance: “and his wife, carrier of firewood” is then a circumstantial clause referring to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (سيصلى), or “his wife” is conjoined to that pronoun. On the earlier interpretation it cannot be a circumstantial clause, but one sentence is conjoined to another. Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Imraʾatuhu has two analyses: a subject with ḥammāla (حمّالة) as predicate; or conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (سيصلى). On the latter, ḥammāla (حمّالة) is either an adjective for the preceding noun or stands for ‘while she is a carrier.’”\n\nEnd of the sūrah.","title":"Anger, the poem, and final syntax [XVI:630–631]","author":"tibi","on":["K111.05"],"anchor":"K111.05","surah":111,"ar":"قولُه: (لِتَمْتعض)، مَعِضْتُ من ذلك الأمرِ أَمعضُ معضًا، وامتعضتُ منه، إذا غضبتَ وشقَّ عليك.\n\nقولُه: (ماذا أَرَدْتَ) البيتين، أَرَدْتَ: أي: مِلْتَ: ضُمّنَ الإرادةُ معنى الميل وعُدِّي بإلى. الشَّادخة: الغُرَّةُ التي فَشَتْ في الوجهِ من الناصيةِ إلى الأنفِ ولم تُصبْ العينين، يوصفُ بها كرائمُ الخيل. والمرادُ بالشيخِ عبدُ المطلبِ وليسَ به؛ لأنها بنتُ حربٍ، أُختُ أبي سفيانَ كما ذكره.\n\nقولُه: (ويُحتملُ أن يكونَ المعنى أن حالها تكونُ في نارِ جهنّمَ على الصورةِ التي كانت عليها)، فعلى هذا: ﴿وَامْرَأَتُهُ حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ﴾، الجملةُ حالٌ من الضميرِ في ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾، أو يعطفُ ﴿وَامْرَأَتُهُ﴾ على الضمير. وعلى الأولِ لا يجوزُ الحال، بل عطفُ جملةٍ على جملة، قالَ أبو البقاء: «(امرأتُه) فيه وجهان: أحدهما مبتدأٌ والخبرُ حَمّالة\"، وثانيهما هو معطوفٌ على الضمير في ﴿سَيَصْلَى﴾؛ فعلى هذا، في \"حَمّالة\" وجهان: أحدهما نعتٌ لِما قبلَه، والثاني تقديرُه: وهي حَمّالة».\n\nتَمَّتِ السُّورة","html":"<p>Maʿiḍtu and imtaʿaḍtu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">امتعضت</bdi>) mean I became angry and found something distressing. In “what did you intend toward insulting me,” aradta (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أردت</bdi>) includes the meaning of turning toward, and therefore takes ilā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلى</bdi>). A shādikha (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">شادخة</bdi>) is a horse’s broad white blaze extending from forelock to nose without touching the eyes, a feature of noble horses. “The elder” is taken as ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib—but this is not correct, since she is Ḥarb’s daughter and Abū Sufyān’s sister, as stated.</p><p>On the interpretation that her appearance in Hell matches her former appearance: “and his wife, carrier of firewood” is then a circumstantial clause referring to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>), or “his wife” is conjoined to that pronoun. On the earlier interpretation it cannot be a circumstantial clause, but one sentence is conjoined to another. Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Imraʾatuhu has two analyses: a subject with ḥammāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالة</bdi>) as predicate; or conjoined to the pronoun in sa-yaṣlā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سيصلى</bdi>). On the latter, ḥammāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حمّالة</bdi>) is either an adjective for the preceding noun or stands for ‘while she is a carrier.’”</p><p>End of the sūrah.</p>","lemma":"لتمتعض","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/short-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"111:5","number":5,"ar":"فِى جِيدِهَا حَبْلٌ مِّن مَّسَدٍۭ","en":"Will have upon her neck a halter of palm-fibre"}],"quran_ar":"فِى جِيدِهَا حَبْلٌ مِّن مَّسَدٍۭ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"5. Will have upon her neck a halter of palm-fibre"}];
